 ## Proof
 
 **Proof technique:** identify the relative Thom pullback and then forget supports before applying normal Thom duality.
 
-1.1 By [F1], $Z$ has codimension $r$ and normal bundle $E|_Z$ with the prescribed orientation. By [F5] every bundle and base used here meets the Thom hypotheses. Let $\alpha\in H^r(M,M\setminus Z;R)$ be its normal Thom extension and $\bar\alpha$ its absolute image. By [F3], $\bar\alpha=e(E)$. [F1, F3, F5, given]
+1.1 If $Z=\varnothing$, the relative group $H^r(M,M\setminus Z;R)=H^r(M,M;R)$ is zero by [F6]. The relative Thom pullback of [F3] therefore has zero absolute image, so $e(E)=0$. Its cap with $[M]$ is zero, and the empty pushforward in the displayed formula is the zero class as specified in [F6], also when $r>n$ and the target homology degree is negative. No normal-duality theorem is invoked with a negative-dimensional submanifold. Both nonvanishing assertions are false in this case. Henceforth assume $Z\ne\varnothing$. [F3, F6, given, cases, algebra]
 
-2.1 Apply [F2] with $z=n-r$ to obtain $e(E)\cap[M]=(-1)^{r(n-r)}(i_Z)_*[Z]$. If $Z$ is empty its relative group is zero and [F3] gives $e(E)=0$, including $r>n$, when transversality forces the zero locus to be empty. Cap duality [F4] gives the stated equivalence of nonzero classes. [F2, F3, F4, step 1.1, algebra]
+2.1 At an actual zero, transversality gives a surjection $T_xM\to E_x$, so $r\le n$; [F1] now gives the closed embedded $(n-r)$-manifold $Z$ and its normal bundle $E|_Z$ with the prescribed orientation. By [F5] every bundle and base used here meets the Thom hypotheses. Let $\alpha\in H^r(M,M\setminus Z;R)$ be its normal Thom extension and $\bar\alpha$ its absolute image. By [F3], $\bar\alpha=e(E)$. [F1, F3, F5, step 1.1, given]
 
-3.1 If $r=n$, each zero is nondegenerate, and its point orientation is the sign of $D^vs:T_xM\to E_x$ in the supplied orientations. The cap formula of [F4] followed by augmentation therefore gives $\langle e(E),[M]\rangle=\sum_{x\in Z}\operatorname{sgn}(D^vs_x)$. If $r=0$, $Z=M$ has dimension $n$; its induced orientation is the ambient orientation multiplied by the supplied rank-zero orientation unit $o$ (over $\mathbb Z$, $o^{-1}=o$). Thus $[Z]=o[M]$ and the formula reads $e(0_M,o)\cap[M]=o[M]$, as [F5] requires. For the zero section the vertical derivative is zero at every base point, so it is surjective exactly when $r=0$ if the base is nonempty; there are no points to check when the base is empty. Empty manifolds and the canonical mod-two orientations satisfy the same formulas. AC is inherited from the stated suppliers. [F1, F4, F5, step 2.1, algebra] ∎
+3.1 In this nonempty case apply [F2] with $z=n-r\ge0$ to obtain $e(E)\cap[M]=(-1)^{r(n-r)}(i_Z)_*[Z]$. Together with step 1.1 this proves the formula in every case. Cap duality [F4] gives the stated equivalence of nonzero classes; either nonzero class excludes the empty case by step 1.1. [F2, F3, F4, step 1.1, step 2.1, algebra]
 
+4.1 If $r=n$, each zero is nondegenerate, and its point orientation is the sign of $D^vs:T_xM\to E_x$ in the supplied orientations. The cap formula of [F4] followed by augmentation therefore gives $\langle e(E),[M]\rangle=\sum_{x\in Z}\operatorname{sgn}(D^vs_x)$. If $r=0$, $Z=M$ has dimension $n$; its induced orientation is the ambient orientation multiplied by the supplied rank-zero orientation unit $o$ (over $\mathbb Z$, $o^{-1}=o$). Thus $[Z]=o[M]$ and the formula reads $e(0_M,o)\cap[M]=o[M]$, as [F5] requires. For the zero section the vertical derivative is zero at every base point, so it is surjective exactly when $r=0$ if the base is nonempty; there are no points to check when the base is empty. Empty manifolds and the canonical mod-two orientations satisfy the same formulas. AC is inherited from the stated suppliers. [F1, F4, F5, step 3.1, algebra] ∎
